id: pm-k9m6wc
title: "Published GitHub releases remain mutable, so daily-release immutability is policy-only instead of platform-enforced"
description: "The sole v2026.8.15 GitHub release is correctly unique and its npm artifact is published, but GitHub reports isImmutable:false. GitHub now supports repository-level immutable releases for future publications, locking release assets and tags and producing release attestations. The release system must enable that control and make the immutable state an executable publication/verification contract without creating a second automatic release in one UTC day."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[9]: attestation,bunx,github-actions,immutability,negative-control,npm,npx,release,supply-chain
created_at: "2026-08-15T13:55:57.823Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-15T14:07:05.702Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: "Repository or organization policy enables GitHub release immutability for future releases; publication stages every required asset in a draft before publishing; the release gate verifies the published record is immutable and binds the tag, commit SHA, assets, checksums, and release attestation; a negative control proves the workflow refuses to advertise success when immutability is absent or unverifiable; tag movement, tag deletion, asset mutation, and same-name tag reuse protections are covered by executable evidence where the platform permits; one automatic release per UTC day remains enforced while explicitly manual same-day releases retain their separately governed path; npm latest, npx, bunx, and direct package installation resolve the same version and do not trigger additional publication; recovery behavior is documented for failures before publication because assets cannot be appended afterward; local and hosted gates consume the same SDK contract and emit compact machine-readable receipts; release-health monitoring alerts on policy drift without attempting destructive repair; public documentation explains the immutable-release trust boundary without credentials or host-specific operations."
goal: "Make every published daily release cryptographically and operationally trustworthy across GitHub, npm, npx, and bunx."
objective: "Enable GitHub immutable releases and gate every future publication on verifiable tag, asset, attestation, and consumer identity."
value: Closes the gap between the project's immutable-release promise and the platform state consumers can independently verify.
impact: "Prevents post-publication tag and asset tampering, strengthens provenance, and makes release integrity machine-checkable."
outcome: "Future daily releases report immutable state, carry verifiable attestations, and pass exact-artifact consumer checks without duplicate publication."
why_now: "The live daily release succeeded today but exposed isImmutable:false, and GitHub's supported immutable-release control only protects future publications after it is enabled."
parent: pm-u9d0
risk: high
confidence: high
severity: high
environment: "GitHub.com Releases, GitHub Actions auto-release and recovery workflows, npm registry, npx and bunx consumers"
repro_steps: "Run gh release list with the isImmutable field for v2026.8.15; observe isImmutable:false despite a unique successful daily release. Compare with GitHub's repository immutable-release capability, which applies to future releases."
expected_result: "Every future published GitHub release is platform-immutable and its exact tag, commit, assets, attestation, npm artifact, npx result, and bunx result are verified by one shared SDK-backed gate."
actual_result: "v2026.8.15 is unique and published successfully, but GitHub reports isImmutable:false, so tags and assets lack the platform-enforced immutable-release guarantee."
component: release governance and supply-chain integrity
regression: false
customer_impact: Consumers currently depend on repository permissions and workflow discipline to prevent post-publication tag or asset mutation; the GitHub release record does not supply the strongest available tamper-evident guarantee.
